==English==

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|frm|rupture}}, or its source, {{bor|en|la|ruptūra||a breaking, rupture (of a limb or vein)}} and {{der|en|ML.|ruptūra||a road, a field, a form of feudal tenure, a tax, etc.}}, from the participle stem of {{m|la|rumpo|rumpere|to break, burst}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈɹʌptʃə/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-rupture.wav|Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[burst]], [[split]], or [[break]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=7|passage=Hatch from the egg, that soon, / Bursting with kindly '''rupture''', forth disclosed / Their callow young.}}
# A social [[breach]] or [[break]], between individuals or groups.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|author={{w|Edward Everett}}|title=Claims of the United States on Naples and Holland
|passage=He knew that policy would disincline Napoleon from a '''rupture''' with his family.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1761|title=The Modern Part of an Universal History
|passage=Thus a war was kindled with ''Lubec''; ''Denmark'' took part with the king's enemies, and made use of a frivolous pretence, which demonstrated the inclination of his ''Danish'' majesty to come to a '''rupture'''.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} A break or tear in [[soft tissue]], such as a [[muscle]].
# {{lb|en|engineering}} A [[failure]] mode in which a tough [[ductile]] material pulls apart rather than cracking.

